1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,730
Somewhere in every clothing
shop there is an invisible line.

2
00:00:04,660 --> 00:00:06,980
On one side of it, a sign says MEN.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:09,220
On the other side, WOMEN.

4
00:00:09,860 --> 00:00:11,680
Nobody guards that line.

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:15,440
Nobody asks to see your papers
when you step across it.

6
00:00:16,250 --> 00:00:19,390
And yet almost everybody
knows exactly where it runs.

7
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:23,880
This is men's clothing.
That is women's clothing.

8
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:28,230
This colour is masculine.
That one is feminine.

9
00:00:28,987 --> 00:00:31,047
So somebody must have drawn it.

10
00:00:31,687 --> 00:00:34,067
Some committee. Some international body.

11
00:00:34,147 --> 00:00:36,807
The Ministry of Trousers, perhaps.

12
00:00:37,625 --> 00:00:38,825
There isn't one.

13
00:00:39,539 --> 00:00:41,399
There is no authority
anywhere that decides

14
00:00:41,629 --> 00:00:44,399
what counts as men's clothing and women's.

15
00:00:45,329 --> 00:00:49,049
No committee. No register.
No standard. No inspector.

16
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:53,039
The rules are real enough that
breaking one gets you stared at.

17
00:00:53,119 --> 00:00:55,659
And nobody ever voted on them.

18
00:00:56,269 --> 00:00:58,469
So where exactly do they come from?

19
00:01:00,809 --> 00:01:02,589
Start with the honest part.

20
00:01:03,149 --> 00:01:04,729
Some of the difference is practical.

21
00:01:05,664 --> 00:01:08,264
Bodies are shaped differently.
Cuts follow shapes.

22
00:01:08,494 --> 00:01:10,124
Preferences are real.

23
00:01:10,731 --> 00:01:12,511
But walk back into that shop and look at

24
00:01:12,741 --> 00:01:15,291
what actually separates
the two halves of it.

25
00:01:16,221 --> 00:01:19,441
A different colour. A different
pattern. A different label.

26
00:01:20,135 --> 00:01:21,795
Or simply a different aisle.

27
00:01:22,525 --> 00:01:25,185
Does the fabric know which
aisle it was hung in?

28
00:01:25,935 --> 00:01:29,915
Does a pair of trousers change when
somebody carries it across the shop?

29
00:01:30,825 --> 00:01:32,105
Of course not.

30
00:01:32,948 --> 00:01:34,808
The difference is not in the cloth.

31
00:01:35,388 --> 00:01:37,188
It is something we added to it.

32
00:01:39,258 --> 00:01:42,398
Which leaves a better question
than who invented all this.

33
00:01:43,368 --> 00:01:44,688
Who keeps it alive?

34
00:01:45,378 --> 00:01:47,138
The answer is not one person.

35
00:01:47,218 --> 00:01:49,078
It is not even one industry.

36
00:01:50,028 --> 00:01:51,228
It is a loop.

37
00:01:52,108 --> 00:01:55,588
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

38
00:01:56,208 --> 00:01:57,808
It really helps the channel.

39
00:01:58,998 --> 00:02:01,298
A manufacturer designs a product.

40
00:02:01,378 --> 00:02:04,418
Before anything else, they
have to decide who it is for.

41
00:02:05,238 --> 00:02:07,018
So they look at what people buy.

42
00:02:07,098 --> 00:02:10,438
And people buy men's
things and women's things.

43
00:02:11,168 --> 00:02:14,328
So the manufacturer makes men's
things and women's things.

44
00:02:15,378 --> 00:02:17,518
A retailer has to lay out a shop.

45
00:02:18,398 --> 00:02:20,118
Customers expect two sections.

46
00:02:20,218 --> 00:02:22,478
So the shop gets two sections.

47
00:02:23,438 --> 00:02:25,838
Marketing has to sell whatever arrives.

48
00:02:25,918 --> 00:02:28,178
So it tells us who each thing is for.

49
00:02:28,948 --> 00:02:31,568
Men's. Women's. Masculine. Feminine.

50
00:02:32,052 --> 00:02:35,032
Customers see those categories
everywhere they look.

51
00:02:35,912 --> 00:02:37,832
And they start to expect them.

52
00:02:38,432 --> 00:02:42,592
Then manufacturers study those
expectations, and design for them.

53
00:02:43,472 --> 00:02:45,672
Retailers follow the manufacturers.

54
00:02:46,352 --> 00:02:48,272
Marketing follows the retailers.

55
00:02:48,932 --> 00:02:50,612
People follow the marketing.

56
00:02:51,432 --> 00:02:52,872
And around we go.

57
00:02:53,692 --> 00:02:57,472
People create expectations.
Businesses answer them.

58
00:02:57,991 --> 00:03:01,531
Businesses reinforce
them. People learn them.

59
00:03:02,061 --> 00:03:03,601
And then the businesses
point at the people

60
00:03:03,831 --> 00:03:06,421
and say: that is what customers want.

61
00:03:07,351 --> 00:03:11,871
And the people point at the businesses
and say: that is just how things are.

62
00:03:12,691 --> 00:03:14,251
And the circle closes.

63
00:03:15,133 --> 00:03:16,673
So who started it?

64
00:03:17,253 --> 00:03:18,513
Possibly nobody.

65
00:03:19,461 --> 00:03:21,381
And that is the strange part.

66
00:03:22,001 --> 00:03:24,701
A system like this does
not need anyone in charge.

67
00:03:25,701 --> 00:03:28,921
It only needs everybody
to keep taking part in it.

68
00:03:31,471 --> 00:03:34,051
Which is where the companies
come into the story.

69
00:03:34,981 --> 00:03:37,081
It would be easy to tell it like this.

70
00:03:37,661 --> 00:03:41,701
Big firms invented gendered clothing
and forced it on the rest of us.

71
00:03:42,571 --> 00:03:44,651
That is not really how it works.

72
00:03:45,531 --> 00:03:49,471
Companies are not standing outside
the culture and aiming at it.

73
00:03:50,301 --> 00:03:53,681
They are selling things to
people who are already inside it.

74
00:03:54,511 --> 00:03:57,931
They watch what we buy. What
we ignore. What we click on.

75
00:03:58,554 --> 00:04:01,834
What makes somebody think:
that one is for people like me.

76
00:04:02,614 --> 00:04:04,614
And if splitting a
product in two sells more

77
00:04:04,844 --> 00:04:07,454
of it, there is every
reason to keep going.

78
00:04:08,454 --> 00:04:10,374
Not because of a grand plan.

79
00:04:10,834 --> 00:04:12,934
Sometimes it is simply good business.

80
00:04:13,944 --> 00:04:16,724
If a label helps sell the
thing, the label stays.

81
00:04:19,204 --> 00:04:21,964
And marketing can make
that split much sharper.

82
00:04:22,944 --> 00:04:26,464
Take a single product. A
razor. A pen. A bicycle.

83
00:04:27,136 --> 00:04:30,496
One advertisement says: POWER.
PERFORMANCE. DURABILITY.

84
00:04:31,596 --> 00:04:34,436
Another says: BEAUTY. ELEGANCE. SOFTNESS.

85
00:04:35,156 --> 00:04:37,876
The same thing. Two
completely different promises.

86
00:04:38,966 --> 00:04:43,486
And suddenly the thing does not just
have a function. It has an identity.

87
00:04:44,526 --> 00:04:46,166
One we are expected to recognise.

88
00:04:47,666 --> 00:04:50,046
Sometimes those differences are useful.

89
00:04:50,646 --> 00:04:52,326
Sometimes they are genuine.

90
00:04:53,206 --> 00:04:56,066
And sometimes the difference
is mostly in our heads.

91
00:04:56,966 --> 00:04:58,726
But once you have seen
the same message a few

92
00:04:58,956 --> 00:05:01,986
thousand times, it stops
feeling like a message.

93
00:05:02,936 --> 00:05:04,836
It starts feeling like a fact.

94
00:05:07,296 --> 00:05:09,116
And this is where we come in.

95
00:05:09,826 --> 00:05:12,346
Picture a man walking past you in a skirt.

96
00:05:13,296 --> 00:05:16,336
Something in your head says:
that is women's clothing.

97
00:05:17,286 --> 00:05:18,486
But why?

98
00:05:19,601 --> 00:05:22,521
Nobody ever sat you down
and explained the rule.

99
00:05:23,441 --> 00:05:27,641
You learned it by watching. Your parents.
Your classmates. Television. Films.

100
00:05:27,721 --> 00:05:28,921
Adverts.

101
00:05:29,297 --> 00:05:31,697
Shop windows. Strangers in the street.

102
00:05:32,457 --> 00:05:34,017
You saw who wore what.

103
00:05:34,637 --> 00:05:37,977
And you saw how people reacted
when somebody crossed the line.

104
00:05:38,997 --> 00:05:42,477
So you learned the map. Men
over here. Women over there.

105
00:05:43,061 --> 00:05:46,981
You do not have to think about
it any more. It runs by itself.

106
00:05:48,071 --> 00:05:52,611
And when somebody breaks it, it can feel
wrong, even when you cannot say why.

107
00:05:53,341 --> 00:05:56,661
So the next time you see a man
wearing a skirt, don't laugh.

108
00:05:57,701 --> 00:05:58,901
Don't stare.

109
00:05:59,653 --> 00:06:02,013
Ask yourself why it feels unusual.

110
00:06:02,863 --> 00:06:05,263
Maybe it's feminine only in our heads.

111
00:06:08,123 --> 00:06:09,903
Because we have a bad habit.

112
00:06:10,403 --> 00:06:12,823
We confuse familiarity with nature.

113
00:06:13,643 --> 00:06:15,943
Something can feel
ancient and obvious simply

114
00:06:16,173 --> 00:06:18,363
because we have seen it our whole lives.

115
00:06:19,453 --> 00:06:21,813
People say men have always worn this.

116
00:06:21,993 --> 00:06:24,193
Women have always worn that.

117
00:06:24,743 --> 00:06:27,403
So how far back does always actually go?

118
00:06:29,503 --> 00:06:32,583
In 1918, an American trade magazine for

119
00:06:32,813 --> 00:06:35,323
children's clothing told
shops the following.

120
00:06:36,013 --> 00:06:40,473
Pink is the stronger, more decided colour,
so it is more suitable for the boy.

121
00:06:41,563 --> 00:06:45,263
Blue is more delicate and dainty,
so it is prettier for the girl.

122
00:06:46,203 --> 00:06:48,723
Exactly the wrong way
round, printed as ordinary

123
00:06:48,953 --> 00:06:52,183
retail advice, just over
a hundred years ago.

124
00:06:53,123 --> 00:06:56,723
And before you conclude that everybody
did it that way, they didn't.

125
00:06:57,363 --> 00:07:00,163
Nine years later, Time
magazine listed what the

126
00:07:00,393 --> 00:07:03,023
big American department
stores were advising.

127
00:07:03,923 --> 00:07:05,623
Some said pink for boys.

128
00:07:06,103 --> 00:07:07,943
Some said exactly the opposite.

129
00:07:08,883 --> 00:07:10,563
They were not following a rule.

130
00:07:10,723 --> 00:07:12,323
There wasn't one yet.

131
00:07:12,803 --> 00:07:17,923
The version we grew up with did not
settle in until about the 1950s.

132
00:07:19,573 --> 00:07:21,073
Or take high heels.

133
00:07:21,863 --> 00:07:24,083
They reached Europe from
Persia, where cavalry

134
00:07:24,313 --> 00:07:26,203
wore them to brace against the stirrups.

135
00:07:27,249 --> 00:07:30,729
In the 1600s in Europe,
a heel was a man's shoe.

136
00:07:31,709 --> 00:07:35,009
A sign of status. A sign of masculinity.

137
00:07:35,919 --> 00:07:39,159
At the French court, a red heel
meant you were close to the king.

138
00:07:40,129 --> 00:07:42,709
Nobody at the time thought
heels were feminine.

139
00:07:43,689 --> 00:07:44,889
Or trousers.

140
00:07:45,748 --> 00:07:49,048
In ancient Rome, a grown man
wore a tunic down to the knee.

141
00:07:50,058 --> 00:07:51,938
Bare legs. No trousers.

142
00:07:52,799 --> 00:07:55,899
If you saw it in the street
today, you would call it a dress.

143
00:07:56,671 --> 00:07:57,971
Rome had a line too.

144
00:07:58,051 --> 00:07:59,751
It just ran somewhere else.

145
00:08:00,681 --> 00:08:04,581
Their line was length. A man's
tunic stopped at the knee.

146
00:08:05,221 --> 00:08:06,881
A longer one got him called effeminate.

147
00:08:08,163 --> 00:08:12,363
And trousers were what foreigners
wore. Gauls. Germans. Persians.

148
00:08:13,059 --> 00:08:15,619
In 397, that became law.

149
00:08:16,519 --> 00:08:18,919
No trousers inside the city of Rome.

150
00:08:19,519 --> 00:08:21,099
The penalty was exile.

151
00:08:22,622 --> 00:08:24,902
Paris had an ordinance
requiring a woman to

152
00:08:25,132 --> 00:08:27,462
ask the police for
permission to wear them.

153
00:08:28,492 --> 00:08:30,272
A doctor's note as well.

154
00:08:30,552 --> 00:08:32,152
Renewed every six months.

155
00:08:32,842 --> 00:08:34,962
It was issued in 1800.

156
00:08:35,861 --> 00:08:38,161
A French senator asked about it.

157
00:08:38,621 --> 00:08:40,721
The government answered in 2013.

158
00:08:41,719 --> 00:08:44,939
It had quietly lost all
force a very long time ago.

159
00:08:45,879 --> 00:08:50,939
Not repealed. Just left behind by
everything that had changed around it.

160
00:08:51,839 --> 00:08:53,779
And yes, that was a real law.

161
00:08:54,549 --> 00:08:57,969
You have just seen two of
them. One banned trousers.

162
00:08:58,649 --> 00:09:00,329
The other made you ask permission.

163
00:09:01,149 --> 00:09:03,649
But look at what that one never had to do.

164
00:09:04,479 --> 00:09:07,559
It never had to explain
why trousers were men's.

165
00:09:08,289 --> 00:09:10,169
Everybody already agreed.

166
00:09:10,709 --> 00:09:13,789
The law was written on top of
something that was there first.

167
00:09:14,749 --> 00:09:19,029
Laws like that enforce a
convention. They don't invent it.

168
00:09:19,659 --> 00:09:22,639
And when the convention
moved, the law just sat there.

169
00:09:25,339 --> 00:09:27,339
Fashion changes constantly.

170
00:09:28,467 --> 00:09:32,547
Colours change. Silhouettes
change. Hair changes. Shoes change.

171
00:09:33,211 --> 00:09:35,651
Things that were
masculine become feminine.

172
00:09:36,591 --> 00:09:39,071
Things that were feminine
become masculine.

173
00:09:40,021 --> 00:09:42,261
The boundaries move. Sometimes slowly.

174
00:09:42,341 --> 00:09:44,001
Sometimes very fast.

175
00:09:44,767 --> 00:09:46,507
And while we are living
inside one particular

176
00:09:46,737 --> 00:09:48,907
version of them, they feel permanent.

177
00:09:51,627 --> 00:09:54,427
Now, here is the part
that is easy to get wrong.

178
00:09:55,337 --> 00:09:59,117
Saying that a convention is
invented does not make it imaginary.

179
00:10:00,297 --> 00:10:02,397
The consequences are very real.

180
00:10:03,137 --> 00:10:05,697
A man in a skirt can still be laughed at.

181
00:10:06,527 --> 00:10:10,587
A woman in something considered
masculine can still be judged for it.

182
00:10:11,417 --> 00:10:14,197
A child can be told that
a colour is for girls.

183
00:10:15,147 --> 00:10:19,307
An adult can feel embarrassed buying
something from the wrong half of the shop.

184
00:10:20,297 --> 00:10:21,857
None of that is law.

185
00:10:22,157 --> 00:10:24,397
No police force enforces it.

186
00:10:24,918 --> 00:10:26,818
The enforcement is social.

187
00:10:27,574 --> 00:10:30,274
Embarrassment. Ridicule. Disapproval.

188
00:10:30,849 --> 00:10:32,809
The raised eyebrow. The joke.

189
00:10:33,459 --> 00:10:35,619
The question: why are you wearing that?

190
00:10:36,249 --> 00:10:40,129
And most of the time, just the quiet
fear of what other people will think.

191
00:10:43,109 --> 00:10:47,609
But if all of this is held up by a loop,
then the loop can also turn the other way.

192
00:10:48,699 --> 00:10:52,459
Somebody does something
unusual. People see it.

193
00:10:53,109 --> 00:10:56,129
And somebody else thinks:
actually, I like that.

194
00:10:56,599 --> 00:10:58,879
They try it. Other people see them.

195
00:10:59,559 --> 00:11:02,199
The strange thing becomes
slightly less strange.

196
00:11:03,219 --> 00:11:04,859
More people try it.

197
00:11:05,159 --> 00:11:06,719
Shops notice the demand.

198
00:11:07,541 --> 00:11:09,601
Manufacturers answer it.

199
00:11:10,041 --> 00:11:12,101
Marketing changes its language.

200
00:11:12,771 --> 00:11:16,211
The thing becomes easier to
find, so more people see it.

201
00:11:16,981 --> 00:11:20,561
And eventually, what looked
ridiculous looks completely ordinary.

202
00:11:21,691 --> 00:11:23,211
The convention changed.

203
00:11:24,133 --> 00:11:27,013
Not because anybody
officially changed the rules.

204
00:11:28,043 --> 00:11:31,383
Because enough people stopped
treating the old ones as absolute.

205
00:11:34,033 --> 00:11:37,273
Which brings us back to the
question on the wall of that shop.

206
00:11:38,273 --> 00:11:40,793
Is this men's clothing
or women's clothing?

207
00:11:41,733 --> 00:11:44,333
Sometimes that is simply
the wrong question.

208
00:11:45,323 --> 00:11:48,903
A better one might be: what
is this clothing actually for?

209
00:11:49,563 --> 00:11:53,103
Does it fit? Is it comfortable?
Does it do the job?

210
00:11:53,673 --> 00:11:56,073
Do you like it? Does
it make you feel good?

211
00:11:56,813 --> 00:12:00,113
If the answer is yes, what
exactly is the label adding?

212
00:12:01,003 --> 00:12:03,543
And now the distinction that matters most.

213
00:12:04,383 --> 00:12:07,943
Nobody is saying that men and
women have to dress identically.

214
00:12:08,893 --> 00:12:10,213
People are allowed preferences.

215
00:12:10,303 --> 00:12:12,693
Cultures are allowed traditions.

216
00:12:12,923 --> 00:12:15,853
Fashion is allowed categories.

217
00:12:16,593 --> 00:12:18,793
There is nothing wrong with any of that.

218
00:12:19,653 --> 00:12:21,733
The trouble starts when
a category stops being

219
00:12:21,963 --> 00:12:24,453
a description and turns
into a restriction.

220
00:12:25,743 --> 00:12:29,083
When women's clothing stops meaning
clothing usually marketed to

221
00:12:29,313 --> 00:12:33,407
women, and starts meaning clothing
you are not allowed to wear.

222
00:12:34,307 --> 00:12:36,747
Those are two completely
different statements.

223
00:12:37,747 --> 00:12:39,787
One of them describes a convention.

224
00:12:40,387 --> 00:12:42,107
The other one builds a cage.

225
00:12:45,087 --> 00:12:48,287
So who decides what is
men's and what is women's?

226
00:12:49,047 --> 00:12:51,387
Manufacturers have a part in it.

227
00:12:51,807 --> 00:12:53,087
Retailers have a part.

228
00:12:53,167 --> 00:12:54,827
Advertisers have a part.

229
00:12:55,460 --> 00:12:57,900
Designers. Our families. Our friends.

230
00:12:58,560 --> 00:12:59,980
And so do we.

231
00:13:00,725 --> 00:13:03,405
Sometimes on purpose. Usually not.

232
00:13:04,085 --> 00:13:07,465
Which is why there probably
isn't a single person to blame.

233
00:13:08,545 --> 00:13:10,045
There doesn't have to be.

234
00:13:10,405 --> 00:13:13,205
The system keeps going
because it copies itself.

235
00:13:14,205 --> 00:13:16,545
We learn the categories. We expect them.

236
00:13:17,045 --> 00:13:18,985
Companies answer the expectation.

237
00:13:20,045 --> 00:13:23,685
Their products and their advertising
teach the categories again.

238
00:13:24,655 --> 00:13:27,255
And the next generation
learns them from us.

239
00:13:28,035 --> 00:13:29,435
Round and round.

240
00:13:32,065 --> 00:13:34,625
But a convention has
one enormous weakness.

241
00:13:35,655 --> 00:13:38,815
It only works as long as
people believe that it matters.

242
00:13:39,815 --> 00:13:42,555
Which means you don't have to
bring the whole thing down.

243
00:13:43,455 --> 00:13:45,515
You don't have to convince anybody.

244
00:13:46,415 --> 00:13:49,735
You don't even have to stop
wearing men's or women's clothing.

245
00:13:50,725 --> 00:13:54,145
You only have to remember what
these categories actually are.

246
00:13:55,015 --> 00:13:56,315
They are conventions.

247
00:13:57,325 --> 00:14:00,685
Not laws. Not physics.
Not biology. Not morality.

248
00:14:01,465 --> 00:14:02,665
Just conventions.

249
00:14:04,965 --> 00:14:09,505
And if one day you wear something that
convention says belongs to the other side,

250
00:14:10,095 --> 00:14:11,615
you haven't committed a crime.

251
00:14:11,695 --> 00:14:13,795
You haven't broken reality.

252
00:14:13,875 --> 00:14:15,515
You haven't hurt anybody.

253
00:14:16,395 --> 00:14:18,355
You crossed an invisible line.

254
00:14:19,105 --> 00:14:21,605
And maybe the line was never really there.

255
00:14:22,365 --> 00:14:25,985
Maybe we just kept drawing
it. Over and over again.

256
00:14:26,805 --> 00:14:29,645
Until we forgot that we were
the ones holding the pencil.

257
00:14:32,545 --> 00:14:37,635
And if you ever see a skirt you actually
like, why not dare to wear one yourself?
